content.thinking:
The capability table: Learn and Tutor share `interactiveTeaching: true`; Exam has `interactiveTeaching: false` ✓. So `modeCan(session.mode, "interactiveTeaching")` is exactly "modes that teach interactively and may save source-grounded teaching notes" — which is precisely the set whose notes saves schedule unit audits (Learn + Tutor). 

Let me replace:
```ts
const savedDraft = session.mode === "learn" || session.mode === "tutor" ? await loadBook(book.id) : undefined;
```
with
```ts
const savedDraft = modeCan(session.mode, "interactiveTeaching") ? await loadBook(book.id) : undefined;
```
And the following condition `(session.mode === "tutor" || ("objectives" in savedTarget && savedTarget.learnQuality))` — hmm, is that a mode disjunction? The regex requires `mode === "x" || ... mode === "y"` on one line. Mine is `session.mode === "tutor" || ("objectives" in savedTarget && ...)` → the second operand isn't a mode comparison → not flagged ✓. But semantically, it's cleaner to write it as: schedule when the target has a review record:

```ts
if (savedDraft && savedTarget && ports.isActiveAuthority(savedDraft)) scheduleUnitAudits(savedDraft, savedTarget, ctx);
```
